// =============================================================================
// Generic reduction over a type-fn-defined shape family (Slice 3a demo)
//
// The Conal-Elliott "generic parallel algorithm" pattern in miniature (see
// generic-parallel-fe-sketch.fe). ONE generic algorithm is stated over a whole
// FAMILY of shapes built by a `recursive type fn`, and its trait obligation is
// discharged BY THE INDUCTION ENGINE, with NO hand-written `where` bound.
//
// This file is the readable artifact; the compiled-and-asserted mirror is the
// `DEMO` fixture + tests in
//   crates/hir/src/analysis/ty/type_fn_induct.rs
// (search `Slice 3a`). Everything below type-checks green there.
//
// What the pipeline does, end to end:
//   parser            : `recursive type fn RPow<F, const N: usize>() -> (*)`
//   ground normalize  : `RPow<Pair, 3>` -> `Comp<Comp<Comp<Par, Pair>, Pair>, Pair>`
//   symbolic induction: `RPow<F, N>: Reduce` discharged from `F: Reduce` alone
// =============================================================================


// --- The combinators the shapes are built from. -----------------------------
struct Par {}
struct Pair {}
struct Comp<F, G> {}


// --- The reduction marker: a CONSTRAINED trait family. -----------------------
//
// A single leaf `Par` reduces. A composite `Comp<A, B>` reduces ONLY when both
// halves reduce. That constraint on the combinator impl is what makes the
// induction hypothesis LOAD-BEARING: a blanket `impl<A, B> Reduce for Comp<A, B>`
// would discharge every induction step vacuously, and the demo would prove
// nothing. Here `impl<A: Reduce, B: Reduce> Reduce for Comp<A, B>` forces the
// engine to actually carry `Reduce(inner)` through the recursion.
trait Reduce {}
impl Reduce for Par {}
impl Reduce for Pair {}
impl<A: Reduce, B: Reduce> Reduce for Comp<A, B> {}


// --- The carrier that raises the obligation. ---------------------------------
//
// A `Reducer<S>` may only be formed for a shape `S` that reduces. Naming it in a
// signature is the application site whose well-formedness must discharge
// `S: Reduce`.
struct Reducer<S> where S: Reduce {}


// --- The shape family: right- and left-associated functor exponentiation. ----
//
// `RPow<Pair, N>` and `LPow<Pair, N>` are both perfect binary trees of 2^N
// leaves, built from the same `Comp`/`Par` combinators, differing only in
// association (top-down vs bottom-up). One `recursive type fn` each; the
// compiler checks termination on the decreasing subject `N`.
recursive type fn RPow<F, const N: usize>() -> (*) {
    match N {
        0 => Par
        _ => Comp<RPow<F, {N - 1}>, F>
    }
}

recursive type fn LPow<F, const N: usize>() -> (*) {
    match N {
        0 => Par
        _ => Comp<F, LPow<F, {N - 1}>>
    }
}


// --- THE PAYOFF: one generic algorithm over the whole shape family. ----------
//
// Neither signature carries `where RPow<F, N>: Reduce` / `where LPow<F, N>:
// Reduce`. The obligation raised by `Reducer<..>` is discharged by the induction
// engine from `F: Reduce` alone: base arm `Reduce(Par)`, step arm
// `Reduce(Comp<.., F>)` under the induction hypothesis + `F: Reduce`.
fn reduce_rpow<F: Reduce, const N: usize>(x: Reducer<RPow<F, N>>) {}
fn reduce_lpow<F: Reduce, const N: usize>(x: Reducer<LPow<F, N>>) {}


// --- Negative twins (rejected; see the tests). -------------------------------
//
// (A) Drop `F: Reduce` and `reduce_rpow` is rejected: the step arm's `Reduce(F)`
//     subgoal is UnSat, the engine proves nothing, the trait-bound error fires.
//         fn reduce_rpow<F, const N: usize>(x: Reducer<RPow<F, N>>) {}
//
// (B) Remove `impl<A: Reduce, B: Reduce> Reduce for Comp<A, B>` and, even WITH
//     `F: Reduce`, `reduce_rpow` is rejected: nothing reduces a `Comp`, so the
//     induction step is UnSat. The discharge genuinely rides the combinator
//     impl, not a standing/blanket fact.


// --- Ground cross-check (the discharge is real, and it is the engine). -------
//
// `RPow<Pair, 3>` normalizes to `Comp<Comp<Comp<Par, Pair>, Pair>, Pair>`, and
// `Reduce` holds at the ground level via the SAME real impl whether asked of the
// un-normalized application or its normal form. Symbolically, the discharge is
// the induction route: `ImplementorOrigin::Assumption` via the engine, NOT a
// blanket impl (the ordinary solver alone returns UnSat on the opaque head) and
// NOT vacuous (without `F: Reduce` the engine declines).
